decisions
---------

ADR-001: HTTP capture is owned by mitmproxy.

    Context

        collector requires a complete record of HTTP requests and responses.

        HTTP capture should remain independent of browser automation.

    Decision

        mitmproxy is responsible for producing capture.jsonl.

        collector consumes HTTP traffic through the proxy but does not generate
        capture.jsonl itself.

    Consequences

        HTTP capture becomes browser independent.

        Any client capable of using the proxy can produce the same capture
        format.

        collector remains focused on browser automation.


ADR-002: collector owns browser evidence.

    Context

        Browser automation produces information that cannot be obtained from
        HTTP traffic alone.

    Decision

        collector produces actions.jsonl.

        collector produces artifacts.jsonl.

        collector stores collected artifacts on disk.

    Consequences

        Browser interactions remain separate from HTTP capture.

        Downstream tools can correlate browser activity with network traffic.


ADR-003: Collection and analysis are separate concerns.

    Context

        Collecting evidence and interpreting evidence have different
        responsibilities.

    Decision

        collector performs collection only.

        Technology detection, entity extraction, business logic inference,
        vulnerability analysis and reporting belong to downstream tools.

    Consequences

        collector remains deterministic.

        New analysis techniques can be introduced without changing collector.


ADR-004: The crawl frontier is explicit.

    Context

        Recursive browser automation hides crawl state and makes traversal
        difficult to inspect and extend.

    Decision

        collector maintains an explicit frontier.

        URLs are processed as work items rather than recursive browser actions.

    Consequences

        Crawl state becomes observable.

        Breadth-first and future traversal strategies become straightforward to
        implement.


ADR-005: Every page visit has an identity.

    Context

        Browser actions, artifacts and HTTP traffic should be correlated to the
        page that produced them.

    Decision

        Every page visit receives a unique page identifier.

        Browser events reference the page identifier rather than relying solely
        on timestamps.

    Consequences

        Evidence can be correlated reliably.

        Future analysis can reconstruct application behaviour with greater
        precision.


ADR-006: Discovery preserves provenance.

    Context

        URLs may be discovered through many different sources, including HTML,
        JavaScript, redirects, robots.txt, sitemap.xml and future discovery
        mechanisms.

    Decision

        Every discovered URL records how it was discovered.

        Discovery provenance is preserved throughout the crawl.

    Consequences

        Later analysis can determine why a page entered the crawl.

        New discovery mechanisms integrate naturally into the existing
        architecture.


ADR-007: Structured output is versioned.

    Context

        Output formats will evolve over time.

    Decision

        Structured outputs include an explicit schema version.

    Consequences

        Older crawl data remains interpretable.

        Downstream tools can evolve without ambiguity.
